WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.487
Velocity-time graphs often feel crowded because students try to make one feature answer every question.

2
00:00:05.487 --> 00:00:10.975
This lesson fixes that by giving the graph two clear jobs.

3
00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:16.462
Slope tells acceleration because it compares change in velocity with time.

4
00:00:16.462 --> 00:00:21.949
Area under the graph tells displacement because it measures velocity spread across time.

5
00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:27.436
The graph height itself tells the current velocity.

6
00:00:27.436 --> 00:00:32.924
Once those three roles are separated, the graph starts behaving like a motion ledger instead of a puzzle.

7
00:00:32.924 --> 00:00:38.411
Keep this takeaway in view: On a velocity-time graph, height, slope, and area each answer a different motion question.

8
00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:43.898
Height means velocity.

9
00:00:43.898 --> 00:00:49.385
The vertical position tells the current signed velocity.

10
00:00:49.385 --> 00:00:54.873
Slope means acceleration.

11
00:00:54.873 --> 00:01:00.360
How sharply the graph rises or falls tells how quickly velocity is changing.

12
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:07.444
Take the simplest case first: a horizontal line above zero.

13
00:01:07.444 --> 00:01:14.528
Because the line is horizontal, its slope is zero, so the acceleration is zero.

14
00:01:14.528 --> 00:01:21.611
But the velocity is still positive, so motion is still happening.

15
00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:28.695
That means the area under the graph keeps growing as time passes, and that area represents displacement.

16
00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:35.779
This single picture is very powerful because it proves that zero acceleration and non-zero displacement can happen at the same time.

17
00:01:35.779 --> 00:01:42.862
Keep this takeaway in view: A constant velocity section has zero acceleration but still accumulates displacement through its area.

18
00:01:42.862 --> 00:01:49.946
Slope: Horizontal means zero slope, so the acceleration is zero.

19
00:01:49.946 --> 00:01:57.030
Area: A rectangle still forms under the line, so displacement builds up.

20
00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.922
The slope formula now changes meaning because the vertical axis is velocity rather than distance.

21
00:02:01.922 --> 00:02:06.814
Here, slope is change in velocity divided by time.

22
00:02:06.814 --> 00:02:11.706
That is exactly acceleration.

23
00:02:11.706 --> 00:02:16.599
So if one section is steeper than another, the acceleration magnitude is larger on that steeper section.

24
00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:21.491
This is why it is dangerous to memorize slope without checking the graph axes first.

25
00:02:21.491 --> 00:02:26.383
On a distance-time graph, slope meant speed.

26
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:31.275
On a velocity-time graph, slope means acceleration.

27
00:02:31.275 --> 00:02:36.167
Keep this takeaway in view: Axes decide meaning.

28
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:41.059
On a velocity-time graph, slope belongs to acceleration.

29
00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:45.951
Same geometry: A line can be tilted in two graph types, but the meaning changes with the axes.

30
00:02:45.951 --> 00:02:50.844
Graph habit: Name the graph type before you name the slope meaning.

31
00:02:50.844 --> 00:02:55.736
acceleration = slope = Δvelocity / Δtime.

32
00:02:55.736 --> 00:03:00.628
Read the axes first.

33
00:03:00.628 --> 00:03:05.520
The graph type decides what the slope means.

34
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.702
Area becomes much easier when it is read as accumulated motion.

35
00:03:10.702 --> 00:03:15.883
Velocity tells how many metres per second are being gained in a given direction.

36
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:21.065
Time tells how long that velocity lasts.

37
00:03:21.065 --> 00:03:26.246
When you multiply the two, you get metres of displacement.

38
00:03:26.246 --> 00:03:31.428
That is why a rectangle under a constant-velocity section gives displacement directly, and why other shapes can be broken into simpler areas.

39
00:03:31.428 --> 00:03:36.609
The shape under the graph is storing directed motion over time.

40
00:03:36.609 --> 00:03:41.791
Keep this takeaway in view: Area is displacement because velocity sustained through time accumulates into directed motion.

41
00:03:41.791 --> 00:03:46.972
Rectangle idea: Constant velocity times time gives a rectangular area, and that area equals displacement.

42
00:03:46.972 --> 00:03:52.154
Signed meaning: If the graph lies below the axis, the displacement for that region is in the chosen negative direction.

43
00:03:52.154 --> 00:03:57.335
Need acceleration?

44
00:03:57.335 --> 00:04:02.517
Read the slope.

45
00:04:02.517 --> 00:04:07.698
Need displacement?

46
00:04:07.698 --> 00:04:12.880
Read the area.

47
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.497
Signed motion still matters on this graph.

48
00:04:18.497 --> 00:04:24.113
If a section lies below the time axis, the velocity on that section is negative in the chosen sign convention.

49
00:04:24.113 --> 00:04:29.730
That means the motion points in the chosen negative direction.

50
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:35.347
It also means the area there contributes negative displacement rather than positive displacement.

51
00:04:35.347 --> 00:04:40.963
So the graph is not just describing size.

52
00:04:40.963 --> 00:04:46.580
It is also carrying direction through the sign of the velocity.

53
00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:52.197
Keep this takeaway in view: Signed velocity changes the meaning of the area, not just the look of the graph.

54
00:04:52.197 --> 00:04:57.813
Above axis: Positive velocity in the chosen positive direction.

55
00:04:57.813 --> 00:05:03.430
Below axis: Negative velocity in the chosen negative direction.

56
00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:07.665
Now take the classic rectangle example.

57
00:05:07.665 --> 00:05:11.899
A horizontal section sits at 4 metres per second for 5 seconds.

58
00:05:11.899 --> 00:05:16.134
Because the section is horizontal, its slope is zero, so the acceleration is zero.

59
00:05:16.134 --> 00:05:20.368
The area underneath is a rectangle with height 4 and width 5.

60
00:05:20.368 --> 00:05:24.603
Area equals 4 times 5, which gives 20.

61
00:05:24.603 --> 00:05:28.838
So the displacement on that section is 20 metres.

62
00:05:28.838 --> 00:05:33.072
One section, two answers: acceleration zero, displacement 20 metres.

63
00:05:33.072 --> 00:05:37.307
Keep this takeaway in view: Horizontal section -> zero acceleration.

64
00:05:37.307 --> 00:05:41.542
Rectangle area -> displacement.

65
00:05:41.542 --> 00:05:45.776
Velocity level means 4 m/s.

66
00:05:45.776 --> 00:05:50.011
The graph height gives the constant velocity on that section.

67
00:05:50.011 --> 00:05:54.245
Time width means 5 s.

68
00:05:54.245 --> 00:05:58.480
This is the duration of the section on the time axis.

69
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.153
Check the roles quickly.

70
00:06:02.153 --> 00:06:05.826
If the graph asks how velocity is changing, which feature do you read?

71
00:06:05.826 --> 00:06:09.499
The slope.

72
00:06:09.499 --> 00:06:13.171
If the graph asks how much directed motion has built up, which feature do you read?

73
00:06:13.171 --> 00:06:16.844
The area under the graph.

74
00:06:16.844 --> 00:06:20.517
If the section is horizontal above zero, what is the acceleration?

75
00:06:20.517 --> 00:06:24.190
Zero.

76
00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.863
And what still happens to the displacement?

77
00:06:27.863 --> 00:06:31.536
It keeps increasing because the area keeps growing.

78
00:06:31.536 --> 00:06:35.209
Keep this takeaway in view: The graph is calm once the jobs are separated: slope for acceleration, area for displacement.

79
00:06:35.209 --> 00:06:38.881
Need acceleration means read slope.

80
00:06:38.881 --> 00:06:42.554
That is the change-in-velocity feature.

81
00:06:42.554 --> 00:06:46.227
Need displacement means read area.

82
00:06:46.227 --> 00:06:49.900
That is the accumulated-motion feature.

83
00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:55.542
You are ready for the worked example now.

84
00:06:55.542 --> 00:07:01.184
The graph shows a horizontal section at 4 metres per second for 5 seconds.

85
00:07:01.184 --> 00:07:06.826
Start with the slope.

86
00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:12.469
Because the section is horizontal, the slope is zero, so the acceleration is zero metres per second squared.

87
00:07:12.469 --> 00:07:18.111
Then switch jobs and read the area.

88
00:07:18.111 --> 00:07:23.753
The region under the graph is a rectangle, so displacement equals velocity times time, which is 4 times 5.

89
00:07:23.753 --> 00:07:29.395
That gives 20 metres.

90
00:07:29.395 --> 00:07:35.037
So the section has displacement 20 metres and acceleration zero.

91
00:07:35.037 --> 00:07:40.679
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: slope for acceleration, area for displacement, no mixing of jobs.

92
00:07:40.679 --> 00:07:46.321
Step 1: Identify the section shape and read its slope for acceleration.

93
00:07:46.321 --> 00:07:51.964
Step 2: Find the area under that section for displacement.

94
00:07:51.964 --> 00:07:57.606
horizontal line -> slope = 0 -> a = 0 m/s^2.

95
00:07:57.606 --> 00:08:03.248
displacement = area = 4 × 5 = 20 m.

96
00:08:03.248 --> 00:08:08.890
Solve slope and area separately so the graph can answer both questions cleanly.
